{"reference":"1 John 3","book":"1 John","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter holds together a present identity and an unfinished future: its readers are called God’s children now, while what they will be has not yet been made visible. This hope is joined to a life directed toward purity, righteousness, and love rather than to superiority over others.","verse_refs":["1 John 3:1-3"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_003_a6877f6f2cd131f92be6e64c"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Strong Language About Sin","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Verses 6–10 use strikingly absolute language about those who abide in Christ and those born of God not sinning. Elsewhere in the work, claims to be without sin are contradicted, so these statements need to be read together rather than isolated. Readers continue to debate whether the language concerns habitual practice, identity, an ideal, or a future reality; it should not be used to impose perfectionism or shame on people troubled by moral failure.","verse_refs":["1 John 3:24","1 John 3:6-10","1 John 3:9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_003_f914b920db90b5806d4ebbf9"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The chapter defines sin as lawlessness in verse 4, but this definition does not set law against love. Its own central command joins belief in Jesus Christ with loving one another. Likewise, Cain functions as a negative example of hatred between siblings, not as a basis for blaming an inherited group.","verse_refs":["1 John 3:1-3","1 John 3:11-15","1 John 3:19-20","1 John 3:4","1 John 3:6-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_003_a6877f6f2cd131f92be6e64c"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Love in Deed","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The chapter gives love a concrete shape. Jesus’ laying down his life is immediately paired with sharing material goods when a fellow believer is in need; love is therefore expressed not only in speech but “in deed and truth.” The prayer language later in the chapter should not be treated as a promise of material prosperity, guaranteed healing, or a reason to blame those whose requests remain unanswered.","verse_refs":["1 John 3:16-18","1 John 3:21-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_003_7a5297459f0a8b4a08b5e1d2"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"The Condemning Heart","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"In verses 19–20, “God is greater than our heart” may be read either as reassurance when conscience condemns or as a warning of God’s fuller judgment. The wording leaves a real interpretive question, so it should not be pressed in a way that intensifies scrupulosity or mental-health shame.","verse_refs":["1 John 3:1-3","1 John 3:11-15","1 John 3:19-20","1 John 3:4","1 John 3:6-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_003_a6877f6f2cd131f92be6e64c"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"About the Work","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"The work itself does not name its writer, its addressees, their location, or its destination. That limits how specifically readers can reconstruct the setting behind this chapter.","verse_refs":["1 John 1:1-10","1 John 2:1-29","1 John 4:1-21","1 John 5:1-21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_003_3f50ef70b93c1f5a75130e31"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}